---
import BlogPost from "../../layouts/BlogPost.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const serviceCode = `{
  "exec": ["dockerd"],
  "ready": ["docker", "info"],
  "ready_timeout_secs": 60,
  "stop_timeout_secs": 30
}`;

const recipeCliCode = `$ shuru recipe list
NAME     DESCRIPTION
docker   Docker Engine 29.9 with dockerd as a guest service and nginx-unprivileged pre-pulled
browser  Headless Chromium as a guest service, unprivileged user, DevTools protocol on port 9222

$ shuru recipe show docker          # metadata, services and every step, before anything boots
$ shuru run --recipe docker -- docker ps
shuru: building recipe docker from superhq-ai/recipes/docker@6a88421 (2 steps)
shuru: step 1/2: install.sh
shuru: step 2/2: pull.sh
shuru: checkpoint 'docker' saved
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE   COMMAND   CREATED   STATUS   PORTS   NAMES

$ shuru run --recipe docker -- docker ps   # same revision: boots the checkpoint, no rebuild`;

const recipeJsonCode = `{
  "name": "docker",
  "description": "Docker Engine 29.9 with dockerd as a guest service and nginx-unprivileged pre-pulled",
  "min_shuru_version": "0.8.0",
  "disk_size": 4096,
  "memory": 2048,
  "network": {
    "allow": ["deb.debian.org", "security.debian.org", "download.docker.com", "*.docker.io"]
  },
  "services": {
    "docker": {
      "exec": ["dockerd"],
      "ready": ["docker", "info"],
      "ready_timeout_secs": 60,
      "stop_timeout_secs": 30
    }
  },
  "steps": ["install.sh", "pull.sh"]
}`;

const referencesCode = `shuru run --recipe docker -- ...                    # the official catalog
shuru run --recipe owner/repo/path@v1 --yes -- ...  # any GitHub repository, pinned to a tag
shuru run --recipe ./my-recipe -- ...               # a local directory while you write one
shuru run --recipe docker --checkpoint ci -- ...    # pick the checkpoint name yourself`;

const dockerCode = `# Serve the pre-pulled image on host port 8080. No --allow-net needed.
shuru run --from docker -p 8080:8080 -- \\
  docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.31.7-alpine-slim

# Build an image from a mounted project and run it
shuru run --from docker --mount .:/app -- \\
  sh -c 'cd /app && docker build -t app . && docker run --rm app'`;

const browserCode = `# Chromium starts at boot; the command runs once /json/version answers
shuru run --recipe browser -p 9222:9222 -- sleep infinity

# Any CDP client on the host
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9222/json/version

# The browser-use example does exactly this
OPENAI_API_KEY=... uv run --with browser-use --with openai examples/browser-use.py`;

const updateCode = `# Rebuild in place. The old checkpoint stays until the new build exits 0.
shuru checkpoint update base --allow-net -- apt-get install -y build-essential git make

# Layer onto the existing one instead of starting over
shuru checkpoint update base --from base --allow-net -- apt-get install -y jq

# Force a recipe checkpoint to rebuild at the current revision
shuru checkpoint update docker --recipe docker`;

const upgradeCode = `shuru upgrade                    # CLI and OS image
brew upgrade shuru               # if you installed through Homebrew`;
---

<BlogPost slug="recipes-docker-and-a-headless-browser">
  <div class="prose-section">
    <p>
      Until now every sandbox booted as bare Debian. That is the right default for isolation, but the
      environments agents actually need, a Docker daemon, a browser, a toolchain, were yours to script and
      checkpoint by hand. 0.8.0 adds the pieces that make those environments one command, without giving up
      the fresh disk on every run.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four things shipped: guest services, recipes, two official recipes for Docker and a headless browser,
      and <code>checkpoint update</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <h2>Guest services</h2>
    <p>
      The guest has no systemd. PID 1 is a small init written in Rust, which is a large part of why a VM boots
      in about a second. Daemons needed a way to exist in that model, so an image can now declare them in
      <code>/etc/shuru/services/&lt;name&gt;.json</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The init starts every service at boot, polls its readiness check, and only then runs your command.
      On the way down it sends SIGTERM, waits up to the stop timeout, and syncs the disk before the VM
      powers off. The host now asks the guest to shut down over vsock instead of pulling the plug, so a
      checkpoint is always cloned from a consistent image, whichever path stopped the VM.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <CodeBlock code={serviceCode} lang="json" title="/etc/shuru/services/docker.json" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <h2>Recipes</h2>
    <p>
      A recipe is a versioned description of a checkpoint: a <code>recipe.json</code> with metadata and
      services, plus shell steps. Each step runs in a fresh boot from the previous step, so a service
      declared in the recipe is already running from step two onwards. The result is a normal checkpoint
      with one extra file next to it that records where it came from and at which revision.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>shuru run --recipe</code> builds the checkpoint on first use and boots it on every run after.
      References to GitHub resolve to a commit SHA, which is printed and recorded. Change the recipe and
      the next run rebuilds; leave it alone and the next run boots in seconds.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <CodeBlock code={recipeCliCode} lang="bash" title="terminal" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <p>
      The official catalog lives in <a href="https://github.com/superhq-ai/recipes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">superhq-ai/recipes</a>.
      A bare name always means that catalog. Any GitHub repository works too, and a local directory while you
      write one. Third-party recipes print their source and ask before building; official and local ones do not.
      Builds use the network allowlist the recipe declares, never your project <code>shuru.json</code>, and a
      recipe with no allowlist builds offline.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      <CodeBlock code={recipeJsonCode} lang="json" title="recipe.json" />
      <CodeBlock code={referencesCode} lang="bash" title="references" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <h2>Docker inside the VM</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>docker</code> recipe installs pinned docker-ce and containerd, declares dockerd as a guest
      service, and pre-pulls one image so the checkpoint is useful offline. Containers get the same boundary
      as any guest process: no egress without <code>--allow-net</code>, the host allowlist applied by the
      proxy, host ports only through <code>-p</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      To make dockerd happy the guest kernel gained cgroup v2 controllers, cgroup BPF, seccomp filters,
      veth and the netfilter targets Docker installs, and moved to Linux 6.12.114. The init now mounts
      cgroup2 and a tmpfs on <code>/run</code>, which also means <code>/run</code> no longer lands in
      checkpoints. Docker isolation inside the VM stays at its defaults; there is no reason to weaken the
      inner layer when the outer one is a VM.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <CodeBlock code={dockerCode} lang="bash" title="terminal" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <h2>A headless browser for agents</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>browser</code> recipe installs Chromium from Debian and runs it headless as an unprivileged
      user, with the DevTools protocol on port 9222 inside the VM. Forward that port and point Playwright,
      Puppeteer or browser-use at it. The browser, its profile and everything it downloads stay inside the
      microVM. Only the CDP port reaches the host, and only when you forward it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The kernel ships user namespaces, so the Chromium sandbox works as designed on top of the VM boundary
      rather than being switched off. The Browser Use example in the repository now runs on this recipe.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <CodeBlock code={browserCode} lang="bash" title="terminal" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <h2>checkpoint update</h2>
    <p>
      Replacing a checkpoint used to mean delete then create, with nothing in between if the create failed.
      <code>shuru checkpoint update</code> takes the same arguments as <code>create</code>, builds under a
      staging name, and swaps the new disk in only when the command exits 0. A failed update leaves the old
      checkpoint exactly as it was.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <CodeBlock code={updateCode} lang="bash" title="terminal" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <h2>Upgrading</h2>
    <p>
      The CLI pulls the new kernel and rootfs with the release. In the Rust SDK, <code>Sandbox::stop()</code>
      is now the graceful stop that lets services shut down and <code>Sandbox::kill()</code> is the old hard
      stop. The crates are on crates.io as shuru-proto 0.4.0, shuru-proxy 0.3.0, shuru-vm 0.4.0 and
      shuru-sdk 0.5.0. The agent skill in the repository knows about all of the above, so agents that use
      shuru through it pick up recipes without any prompting.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-section">
    <CodeBlock code={upgradeCode} lang="bash" title="terminal" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose-section">
    <p>
      The catalog has two recipes today. If there is an environment your agents keep rebuilding by hand,
      a recipe for it is a directory with a <code>recipe.json</code> and a shell script, and
      <code>shuru run --recipe ./that-directory</code> is the whole test loop. Pull requests to the catalog
      are open.
    </p>
  </div>
</BlogPost>
